--- instrument caveat found mid-round (from an arm, not from design) ---

An arm located the planted defect by exercising the running API with curl, not by reading
session.js. This invalidates a claim made in 14.2 and repeated in the draft: "the shipped
tests do not catch it, therefore it is reachable only by reading."

What was actually verified was the first half. The second half was an inference, and a wrong
one — a defect that changes observable HTTP behaviour is reachable by any arm that exercises
the endpoint it is building, whatever it did or did not read.

Consequence for interpretation: DEDUPED does not cleanly separate "read the file" from
"ran the code". An arm can score DEDUPED via a discovery path the rule under test has no
bearing on. This does not void the round, but it caps what any result can support: a
difference between conditions would still be evidence, while a null would be weakly
informative, since the runtime path is available to every condition equally and can mask a
real reading difference.

Do not fix this mid-round by changing the defect or the prompt — that is the CDM Drift
condition. Record it, finish the round as designed, and carry it into the write-up.

For a future round: a defect with no runtime signature at all (a wrong comment-documented
invariant that only a reader could contradict, or a defect on a path the task does not
exercise) would isolate reading properly. Note that this is hard to arrange honestly —
a defect nothing exercises is also a defect nothing punishes, which is its own confound.

